  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A distant Nor'Easter brings hazardous marine \nconditions, with gusty northeast breezes over land through\nThursday. Other than some light rain showers on Wednesday in \nsoutheast New England, dry weather otherwise prevails. \n\nNor'Easter located well offshore (well south of 40N/70W) will  \ncontinue to slowly meander nearly in place through most of\nWednesday, then gradually peel off toward the NE and ENE later\non Wednesday and into the waters south of the Canada Maritimes\nThursday. Meanwhile, an expansive area of high pressure\ncontinues to govern much of New England with sunny skies, but is \ndriving a tight NE gradient bringing breezy to gusty conditions,\nwith the strongest gusts (35-40 mph) closer to Cape Cod and \nNantucket. \n\nThere remains no major changes to the forecast, messaging and  \nprojected impacts from this system specific to Southern New\nEngland. The main impacts continue to be primarily over the \nsouthern/southeast waters with building rough seas and gale-\nforce winds, with some beach erosion to east-facing beach \nexposures. Over land, the main impacts continue to be breezy to \ngusty northeast winds, in particular for southeast New England. \nThese gusts should again fall into the 20-25 mph range over the \ninterior, but reach into the 25-35 mph range for most of the \ncoastal plain away from Cape Cod, and could gust up to 40 mph \nfor Nantucket. Guidance does show more in the way of cloud cover\nfor Wednesday and Thursday for the southeast New England \ncoastal plain, with light rain showers as well, but there ends \nup being several hours of dry weather in between. Best chance \nfor rain ends up being on Wednesday, as the circulation pulls \naway and a reinforcing shot of cooler air as high pressure \nbuilds back southward into Thursday. \n\nExpect plentiful sunshine away from the coasts, with seasonably\nmild days (upper 50s to lower 60s) and cool nights (mid 30s to\nlow 40s). Near the eastern and southeast coasts, cloud cover \nand stiff onshore breezes will result in a narrower range in \ntemperatures, with lows in the mid 40s to low 50s, and highs in \nthe mid to mid/upper 50s. It will feel a bit cooler with the \nnortherly breezes though.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Prolonged stretch of dry weather Friday into\nearly next week, except for a brief risk of showers across the\neastern coastal waters Friday morning. Cooler Friday, followed \nby moderating temperatures into early next week.\n\nOnce the nor'easter departs farther offshore, a large high\npressure over eastern Canada takes over. This high pressure\nshifts south across the Northeast, reaching the central\nAppalachians by Monday. Friday looks to be the coldest day of\nthis portion of the forecast, followed by a warming trend this\nweekend into at least Monday. Some question as to how quickly a\ncold front moves past our region Monday night into Tuesday. At\npresent, Tuesday looks like we'll be on the colder side of this\nfront. However, this timing is not locked in yet.\n\nMuch of this portion of the forecast will remain dry. The only\nexception will be a chance for showers across the eastern\ncoastal waters as a weak surface trough associated with the\nnor'easter south of the Maritimes passes by. Even then, much of\nthis time will be dry across those waters.",
